3.3 Rule-based AI (assess)

For each asset, assess() returns a status (ok, warn, crit), a risk score from 0 to 100, a detection message, a prediction and a recommended action.

Example for water quality:

Check	Warning	Critical
pH	below 6.8	below 6.5 or above 8.5
TDS	above 400	above 500
Turbidity	above 3.5	above 5

Risk score = the largest of three normalized terms, clamped to 0-100: max((TDS - 300) / 6, (Turb - 2) x 15, (7.2 - pH) x 60)

Forecasting uses slope(): the average change per sample over the last few readings. Projecting it forward gives a time-to-threshold (for example, time to dry mark for a well).

Compound risk: combining signals (low drain flow plus rising level suggests a blockage).

Automation rules: a pump stops automatically below 40% well level and rationing starts; a camera that stops sending video raises an alert, report and work order; a dead sensor raises its own alert.

3.4 Presentation layer
Three.js scene: assets coloured by status, moving traffic, animated data packets from assets to offices.
Panels: asset detail with sparkline, scenario controls, work orders, response log.
Role views: Pradhan office monitor, Nagar Nigam monitor, Citizen app, SDM report templates (blank or filled).
Views: whole village, and the Basti (dense settlement) with its own assets.
Guided demo scripts: timed step sequences that fire scenarios and narrate each step.
3.5 Priority in dense settlements

In the Basti, the same fault scores higher because priority scales with severity x people served. A shared tap or open drain that serves many homes outranks the same fault in a sparse area.

